    "narrative": "For today's slate, **Mickey Moniak** (75.75) and **James Wood** (75.25) stand out as our elite anchors. **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a high-ceiling matchup against Avila, fueled by a significant 14% platoon advantage and park factors that reward his aggressive approach. **James Wood** remains a must-start, as his elite 0.424 xwOBA and top-of-the-order positioning provide an excellent floor despite a lefty-lefty matchup. **Tyler Soderstrom** (67.21) and **Austin Riley** (67.16) also grade out as strong plays; **Tyler Soderstrom** thrives in a favorable park environment, while **Austin Riley** benefits from the pitcher's slow delivery, which creates opportunistic stolen base potential that we cannot ignore given our focus on speed scarcity.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Isaac Paredes** (48.51). While his plate discipline and platoon advantage against right-handed pitching normally command respect, the daunting matchup against deGrom\u2019s 3.08 xERA creates a significant \"SP Skill\" penalty that suppressed his score just below our utility threshold. We are also monitoring the risk profile of **Jose Caballero** (59.97); although he is a strong play, the wind blowing in at his stadium creates a drag on his power output that nearly pushed him into the marginal category. Similarly, **Corbin Carroll** (62.09) and **Seiya Suzuki** (55.25) face tough southpaw assignments today; while **Corbin Carroll** retains start-worthy status due to his speed and leadoff role, **Seiya Suzuki** is battling a significant wind-in warning that tempers our expectations for his total production.",
